`display: contents` does **not** prevent pseudo-elements from generating boxes. The element itself generates no box, but its children and pseudo-elements still generate boxes normally. Its `::before` and `::after` are treated as generated before and after the element’s children and are placed in the parent’s principal box.

```css
.wrapper {
  display: contents;
}

.wrapper::before {
  content: "Before"; /* generates a box */
}

.wrapper::after {
  content: "After";  /* generates a box */
}
```

A `::before` or `::after` pseudo-element will not generate a box if its computed `content` is `none`; otherwise, its default `display: inline` generates an inline box.

Sources: [CSS Display Module Level 3](https://www.w3.org/TR/css-display/), [CSS Pseudo-Elements Module Level 4](https://www.w3.org/TR/2016/WD-css-pseudo-4-20160607), [MDN `content`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/content).
